/// A bound on a type parameter is written in a `where` clause. The inline form
/// `<T: Bound>` is not syntax: a `:` in generic-parameter position is rejected
/// by the parser, which is what this project pins.
///
/// WHY A COMPILE-FAIL PROJECT AND NOT A UNIT TEST
///
/// The rejection happens in `parse_generic_params`, so the file cannot parse -
/// there is no way to assert it from inside a test function in the same
/// package. A project whose declared outcome is `compile_fail` is the only
/// shape that can hold a file the compiler must refuse.
///
/// WHAT WOULD SILENTLY UNDO THIS
///
/// Re-adding a constraint branch to `parse_generic_params` makes this file
/// parse again. Nothing else in the tree writes the form, so no other corpus
/// would notice; without this project the production could come back and every
/// gate would stay green.
///
/// The `where` spelling of the same bound is exercised across the stdlib and is
/// enforced at call sites by E0306. The two are not interchangeable spellings:
/// `where` is the only one the language has, and it accepts a qualified trait
/// path (`where W: io::Write`), which this form never did.
namespace InlineGenericBound::Main;

type trait Tagged {
    tag(&this) -> i32;
}

type struct Widget {
    v: i32;
}

implement trait Tagged for Widget {
    tag(&this) -> i32 { return this.v; }
}

/// The rejected form. `where T: Tagged` is how this is written.
function tag_of<T: Tagged>(x: &T) -> i32 {
    return x.tag();
}

function main() -> i32 {
    mut w: Widget = Widget { v: 7 };
    return tag_of<Widget>(&w) - 7;
}
